  "worked_solution": "The left Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of subintervals,   ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx$, and  $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nThe endpoints indicate that $a=-2$ and $b=1,$ so the step size is\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-(-2)}{n}=\\frac{3}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the left Riemann sum can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})Δx & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f(a+jΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f(-2+j\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f(-2+\\frac{3j}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}{(-2+\\frac{3j}{n}+2)}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\frac{3}{n}{(\\frac{3j}{n})}^{3} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\frac{3}{n}\\cdot \\frac{27{j}^{3}}{{n}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{{n}^{4}}{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\,{j}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
